.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH murk-exec 1  "exec " 
.SH NAME
murk\-exec \- Run a command with secrets injected as environment variables
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmurk exec\fR [\fB\-\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-tag\fR] [\fB\-\-clean\-env\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-reserved\fR] [\fB\-\-vault\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fICOMMAND\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a command with secrets injected as environment variables
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fI<ONLY>\fR
Inject only these secrets (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fI<TAG>\fR
Filter by tag (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-clean\-env\fR
Start the command with only the injected secrets plus PATH, HOME and TERM on Unix

On Windows, murk keeps PATH and a fixed set of system variables.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-reserved\fR
Allow secrets named like loader, shell or tool variables (PATH, LD_*, GIT_*, MURK_*, ...)
.TP
\fB\-\-vault\fR \fI<VAULT>\fR [default: .murk]
Vault filename
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fICOMMAND\fR>
Command and arguments to run

Put murk options such as `\-\-only` before the command. murk passes everything after the command name to the command, so `murk exec echo hi \-\-only FOO` runs `echo hi \-\-only FOO` and injects every secret.
